{"id": "159876", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=159876", "title": "Hyderabad/North", "text": "North Hyderabad or Secunderabad (Telugu: \u0c38\u0c3f\u0c15\u0c3f\u0c02\u0c26\u0c4d\u0c30\u0c3e\u0c2c\u0c3e\u0c26\u0c4d \"Sikindr\u0101b\u0101d\", Urdu: \u0633\u06a9\u0646\u062f\u0631 \u0622\u0628\u0627\u062f \"Sikandar \u0100b\u0101d\") is the twin city of Hyderabad, north and east of the Hussain Sagar. Despite being a part of Greater Hyderabad, Secunderabad has its own history and culture, separate from Hyderabad. The roads are better maintained and broader, with nice parks, open spaces like the parade ground, and some excellent restaurants.\nUnderstand.\nNamed after the Mir Akbar Ali Khan Sikander Jah, Asaf Jah III, Secunderabad was established in 1806 as a British cantonment to keep the army within striking distance of the Nizam. To this day, it maintains large military presence north of Hussain Sagar, as it is the headquarters of the 54th Infantry Division of the Indian Army. It also hosts the winter office of the President of India at the Rashtrapati Nilayam.\nGet in.\nBy metro.\nSecunderabad is connected with Hyderabad through a metro line. The line starts from the MG Bus Station and ends at the JBS Parade Ground."}
{"id": "159878", "revid": "235382", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=159878", "title": "Demir Hisar", "text": "Demir Hisar (Macedonian: \u0414\u0435\u043c\u0438\u0440 \u0425\u0438\u0441\u0430\u0440) is a municipality located in western North Macedonia, south of Kru\u0161evo. The municipality has under 10,000 residents spread across 40 villages and the town of Demir Hisar, which is the municipality's seat. Many of these villages are prime examples of rural Macedonian life.\nUnderstand.\nDemir Hisar Municipality roughly corresponds to the historical region of \u017deleznik, so named for the large amounts of iron ore found in the mountainous area. The municipal center used to be known as Murga\u0161evo but today it is named Demir Hisar, its Turkish name. The municipality is inhabited almost entirely by ethnic Macedonian Orthodox Christians.\nSee.\nSmilevo.\nThe village of Smilevo is known for its importance during the era of the Ilinden Uprising in the early 20th century. The Smilevo Congress, where it was decided to stage uprisings throughout Macedonia, occurred here in early May of 1903. It is also the birthplace of one of the foremost revolutionaries of the time, Dame Gruev."}
