{"id": "160572", "revid": "2441460", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=160572", "title": "Ruijin", "text": "Ruijin (\u745e\u91d1\u5e02) is a small city in the southeastern part of Jiangxi Province at the junction of Jiangxi, Fujian, and Guangdong provinces. It is administered as part of Ganzhou City. Ruijin features a hilly and mountainous landscape with a warm and humid climate and rich natural scenery.\nKnown as the \u201cCradle of the People\u2019s Republic of China\u201d and the \u201cRed Capital\u201d, Ruijin played an important role in the Chinese revolutions of the early 20th century. In 1931, it became the capital of the Chinese Soviet Republic and in 1937 the Long March began here. Today, Ruijin is home to many historical sites and is a major destination for \"red tourism\".\nRuijin has been promoting the integrated development of red tourism, eco-agriculture, and modern industries, working toward balanced economic and cultural progress across urban and rural areas.\nUnderstand.\nVisitors can explore several well-preserved revolutionary heritage sites such as the Former Site of the Central Revolutionary Base, the Memorial of the Chinese Soviet Republic, and Yeping Village, where the first National Congress of the Chinese Soviet was held. These sites allow tourists to gain a deeper understanding of early Communist history and the Red Army\u2019s struggles during China\u2019s revolutionary period.\nBeyond its revolutionary landmarks, Ruijin has also made efforts to balance heritage preservation and moderate tourism development. For example, Shazhouba Village and Yeping Revolutionary Village have been carefully restored to maintain their historical authenticity while providing cultural experiences such as traditional Red Army performances and local handicraft exhibitions. These villages attract visitors who are interested in both the tangible and intangible cultural heritage of early 20th-century China.\nAlthough Ruijin appears close to the large Ganzhou metropolitan area on the map, the city remains relatively secluded by mountains, preserving its tranquil atmosphere and historical charm for visitors seeking a more reflective travel experience.\nHistory.\nRuijin has a remarkable and complex history. Located in southeastern Jiangxi Province, it has long served as a key crossroads between Jiangxi, Fujian, and Guangdong. Over the centuries, the city has experienced several transformative periods \u2014 from a traditional agricultural settlement, to a revolutionary stronghold in the 1930s, and later to a developing regional center in modern China.\nThe most significant period in Ruijin\u2019s history came during the early 1930s, when it became the political and military headquarters of the Chinese Soviet Republic (CSR). Established in 1931 under the leadership of Mao Zedong and Zhou Enlai, the CSR in Ruijin served as the first socialist government on Chinese soil. It was here that many foundational policies of the later People\u2019s Republic of China were first tested, including land reform, local governance structures, and early forms of social welfare.Several historic sites from this era still stand today. The Former Site of the Central Government of the Chinese Soviet Republic in Yeping Village marks the location of the first National Congress of the Chinese Soviet in 1931. The Red Army Hospital, the Printing House, and the Post and Telegraph Office illustrate how the revolutionary base functioned as a fully organized state. The Central Revolutionary Memorial Hall now houses original documents, military equipment, and photographs that trace the evolution of the Red Army and the revolutionary movement. These sites collectively offer valuable insight into the early experimentation with socialist governance and the social ideals of that time.\nFollowing the Long March of 1934, when the Red Army retreated from Ruijin, the city experienced multiple transitions: first into a post-revolutionary rural area recovering from conflict, then into a regional trade hub after the 1980s reform era. Today, while modernization continues, Ruijin\u2019s landscape still bears the marks of its revolutionary past. Its preserved buildings and historical villages serve as living reminders of a formative period in China\u2019s twentieth-century history.\nClimate.\nRuijin has a humid subtropical climate with distinct seasons. The city experiences warm to hot summers and mild winters. Average temperatures range from around 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) in January to 91\u00b0F (33\u00b0C) in July. Rainfall is concentrated mainly between April and June, with each of these months receiving over 2 inches (50 mm) of precipitation, while the winter months are relatively dry. The weather in summer is typically humid, while autumn brings cooler and more comfortable conditions. Overall, Ruijin enjoys a warm climate with abundant rainfall during the first half of the year.\nGet around.\nBy bus.\nRuijin has a well-developed bus network with affordable fares, usually \u00a51\u20132 per ride. Major routes connect the train station, Ruijin Airport, city center, and famous red tourism sites such as Yeping and Shazhouba. It is one of the most convenient and commonly used transportation options for both residents and visitors.\nBy car.\nRuijin has wide and well-maintained roads, making self-driving the most convenient way to visit nearby attractions. It takes about 10\u201320 minutes to drive from downtown to scenic spots like Yeping or Shazhouba, and expressways provide easy access to Ganzhou, Changting, and other surrounding cities.\nBy shared bicycle.\nShared bicycles are available throughout the city, especially around the downtown area, parks, and scenic spots. You can easily rent one by scanning a QR code with your phone. This is good for short-distance travel or leisurely rides.\nEat.\nRuijin offers a wide variety of local and regional cuisines, from traditional Jiangxi dishes to hearty Hakka flavors. Visitors can enjoy authentic tastes that reflect the city\u2019s rich culture and warm hospitality."}
{"id": "160573", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=160573", "title": "Yucca Valley", "text": "Yucca Valley is a large town in south central San Bernadino County in California Desert on the north-western boundary of Joshua Tree National Park.\nUnderstand.\nYucca Valley is at the west edge of the Morongo Basin in the Mojave Desert, between the San Berandino Mountains (west) and Joshua Tree National Park (south).\nGet in.\nBy car.\nMost visitors will arrive by car.\nBy bus.\nThere is a limited bus network, run by the Morongo Basin Transit Authority (MBTA). Service runs west towards Palm Springs and east towards Joshua Tree Village and Twentynine Palms.\nBy plane.\nThe closest passenger airport is Palm Springs Airport (), 35 mi west.\nFurther afield are:"}
